4. Master Speech
One example that comes to mind is a Databricks cost optimisation initiative I led.
When the platform was originally designed, Job Clusters were the recommended architecture for ETL workloads and the solution was performing well.
As the platform matured, I noticed compute costs were steadily increasing, so I took ownership of understanding where the spend was coming from.
Through workload analysis, I discovered that most ETL workloads were SQL-heavy and not Spark-heavy. This prompted me to evaluate whether SQL Warehouse was a better fit.
I ran a proof of concept, compared cost, utilisation and execution performance, and found SQL Warehouse aligned much better with the workload profile.
It reduced repeated startup overhead, improved utilisation and benefited from Photon optimisation.
After presenting the findings and driving stakeholder alignment, we migrated the SQL workloads.
The result was approximately 40% cloud cost reduction, delivering around $300K annual savings while maintaining performance and reliability.
The biggest lesson was that good architecture should continuously evolve as workload patterns and technology capabilities change.
